<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quechua</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Quechuan languages, Runa Simi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quechua is a family of languages spoken by some eight to ten million people in the Andes, chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and Ecuador. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> used it as the administrative language of their empire, and Spanish missionaries promoted it as a common language for preaching. It is an official language of Peru and Bolivia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quechua.jpg" alt="Quechua" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noahedits · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quechuan_distribution_(w_Inca_Empire).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
